# Target C-II — direct dominant/complement outer-energy tradeoff

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Repository base:** after the hostile-audited `g=0.0050` atomic gap and complement outer-train bracing theorem  
**Status:** proof-only full-core Type-II strengthening; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** no-container/full-core Type-II low-`t` branch  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN`

The complement-bracing theorem first bounded the number of dominant-orbit outer
terminal occurrences from above using the joint route cost `Phi^{-1}(delta)`, then
forced the remaining outer terminals into diameter-braced complement trains.  That is
safe but unnecessarily separates two populations which share one exact identity:
**every outer terminal belongs either to the dominant phase windows or to their
complement**.

This note optimizes that split directly.  If the dominant windows use `K D` outer
terminal occurrences, then

- after paying at most `delta D` hull-length capacity loss, those dominant occurrences
  contribute at least
  \[
  F(K-\delta)D,
  \qquad F(z)=z-6\sin(z/6),
  \]
  of actual outer directional energy; while
- the remaining outer occurrences force at least
  \[
  pD=(\pi-K-N_*)D
  \]
  retained complement outer-to-outer train pairs, whose two-sided actual-diameter
  bracing contributes at least
  \[
  J(p)D,
  \qquad J(p)=2p-6\sin(p/3).
  \]

The sum is minimized at a unique interior split.  With the hostile-audited atomic
input

\[
C_0=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0050,
\qquad
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},
\]

the resulting scalar exclusion gives the safe explicit floor

\[
\boxed{
\liminf_{D\to\infty}{q-k\over D}\ge1.83236.
}
\]

The one-variable numerical root of this exact relaxation is

\[
1.8323676277438298975\ldots .
\]

Only the round value `1.83236` is claimed.

---

## 1. Low-`t` scalar region with the current atomic gap

Pass to a convergent full-core Type-II subsequence and write

\[
t={q-k\over D},
\qquad
\kappa={k\over D},
\qquad
\delta={d_{out}\over D}
={b-\operatorname{per}(F)\over D}.
\]

Put

\[
C={T-q/2\over D},
\qquad
C_0=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0050.
\]

The hostile-audited iterative-pruning atomic theorem and the audited §171 upper
envelope give

\[
\boxed{
C_0\le C\le
U:={9t\over2}-2\pi-2\delta-{\kappa\over2}.
}
\tag{DT.1}
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{
2\delta+{\kappa\over2}
\le
R(t):={9t\over2}-2\pi-C_0.
}
\tag{DT.2}

The already-audited joint orbit theorem excludes `t<1.82515`, so below we only need
consider

\[
1.82515\le t\le1.83236.
\tag{DT.3}

---

## 2. Dominant-orbit train mass and outer-terminal lower bound

Use the audited §142/168 exact atomic `pi/3`-orbit decomposition.  If `M` is the
absolute train mass in a dominant orbit and `A` is total absolute atomic train mass,
then

\[
M\ge{4\Delta_2\over A}.
\]

The exposed-hull theorem gives `A<=C`, so

\[
\boxed{
M\ge{4\Delta_2\over C}.
}
\tag{DT.4}

Freeze shrinking disjoint phase windows around the at most three atoms of this orbit.
Let `K D+o(D)` be the number of outer terminal occurrences carried by trains in these
windows.  Every train outside the windows has at most two outer terminal occurrences.
Since

\[
{b\over D}\to\pi+\delta
\]

and

\[
{T\over D}=C+{t+\kappa\over2},
\]

we have

\[
K
\ge
\pi+\delta-t-\kappa-2C+{8\Delta_2\over C}.
\tag{DT.5}

The function

\[
-2C+{8\Delta_2\over C}
\]

is strictly decreasing.  Using `C<=U`,

\[
\boxed{
K\ge K_L:=
\pi+\delta-t-\kappa-2U+{8\Delta_2\over U}.
}
\tag{DT.6}

---

## 3. Complement train population has a simultaneous upper bound

Let `N_c D+o(D)` be the number of complete trains outside the dominant windows.  Then

\[
N_c
={T\over D}-M+o(1)
\le
C+{t+\kappa\over2}-{4\Delta_2\over C}+o(1).
\]

The function

\[
C-{4\Delta_2\over C}
\]

is increasing, hence `C<=U` gives

\[
\boxed{
N_c\le N_*:=
U+{t+\kappa\over2}-{4\Delta_2\over U}.
}
\tag{DT.7}

Equations (DT.6) and (DT.7) use the same dominant-orbit mass estimate in opposite
directions; no independent optimization of `M` is being assumed.

---

## 4. Actual dominant outer occurrences pay `F(K-delta)` energy

For loaded-hull route `W_i`, let

\[
s_i=|b_i-b_{i+1}|,
\qquad
\ell_i=|W_i|,
\qquad
d_i=\ell_i-s_i,
\]

and let `n_i` be the number of dominant-window outer terminal occurrences on this
route.  Retain fractional occurrence weight

\[
m_i=\min\{n_i,s_i\}.
\]

Because `n_i<=ell_i`,

\[
n_i-m_i=(n_i-s_i)_+\le d_i.
\]

Thus the retained dominant occurrence mass is at least

\[
\sum_i m_i/D\ge K-\delta.
\tag{DT.8}

The retained direction measure is dominated by the normalized loaded-hull perimeter
measure.  Type-II near-disk convergence makes the latter tend to Lebesgue measure on
the projective direction circle.  Exactly as in the hostile-audited dominant-orbit
outer-energy theorem, the three-point bathtub minimum gives

\[
\boxed{
E_{dom}\ge F(K-\delta),
\qquad
F(z):=z-6\sin(z/6),
}
\tag{DT.9}

whenever `K>delta`.  This is an **actual weighted sum of directional energies on
outer edge occurrences**, not the joint route relaxation `Phi`.

---

## 5. The remaining outer occurrences force complement two-outer trains

The complement trains carry

\[
\pi+\delta-K
\]

outer terminal occurrences per unit `D`.  If `P_cD+o(D)` complement trains have two
outer terminals, then the other complement trains have at most one, so

\[
\pi+\delta-K
\le N_c+P_c.
\]

Using (DT.7),

\[
P_c
\ge
\pi+\delta-K-N_*.
\tag{DT.10}

Apply the fractional pair-retention argument from the hostile-audited complement
bracing theorem.  At most `delta D` pair weight is lost when both endpoint measures
are truncated to physical hull-side length capacity.  Hence the retained complement
outer-to-outer pair mass obeys

\[
\boxed{
p\ge
\pi-K-N_*.
}
\tag{DT.11}

The metric detour cancels: its `+delta` contribution to the total outer occurrence
count is exactly the maximum pair-retention capacity loss.

For `0<=p<=1`, the three `pi/3` noncrossing phase sectors plus audited §157 two-sided
actual-diameter bracing give

\[
\boxed{
E_{comp}\ge J(p),
\qquad
J(p):=2p-6\sin(p/3).
}
\tag{DT.12}

For `p>1`, retaining only unit pair mass gives the fixed lower bound `J(1)`, which is
already larger than every detour density in (DT.3).  Thus the minimizing regime is
inside `0<p<1`.

---

## 6. The direct split inequality

Dominant-window and complement terminal occurrences belong to disjoint train sets, so
the edge-energy sums in (DT.9) and (DT.12) are disjoint.  The total exact outer
route energy is `delta`.  Therefore the actual dominant occurrence density `K` must
satisfy

\[
\boxed{
\delta
\ge
F(K-\delta)
+J\!\left(\pi-K-N_*\right)
}
\tag{DT.13}

whenever the two displayed arguments are positive.  If the complement argument is
nonpositive, the `J` term is replaced by zero; that large-`K` regime is even more
expensive in `F` on the numerical interval below.

The point of (DT.13) is that `K` is not first bounded above or replaced by an inverse
scalar function.  It is optimized directly.

---

## 7. The split optimization is strictly convex and interior

For fixed `t,delta,kappa`, put

\[
H(K)=F(K-\delta)+J(\pi-K-N_*).
\]

On the active range,

\[
H'(K)
=F'(K-\delta)-J'(\pi-K-N_*),
\tag{DT.14}
\]

where

\[
F'(z)=1-\cos(z/6),
\qquad
J'(p)=2-2\cos(p/3).
\]

Also

\[
H''(K)
={1\over6}\sin((K-\delta)/6)
+{2\over3}\sin((\pi-K-N_*)/3)>0.
\tag{DT.15}
\]

So there is at most one interior minimizer.

The whole feasible region (DT.2)--(DT.3) has the following coarse uniform bounds,
certified by the companion script:

\[
\boxed{
0.9225<N_*<1.083,
\qquad
K_L<1.32,
\qquad
\delta<0.0265.
}
\tag{DT.16}

At `K=1.5`, these imply

\[
H'(1.5)<0,
\tag{DT.17}
\]

while at `K=1.7`,

\[
H'(1.7)>0.
\tag{DT.18}

Hence the unique minimizer satisfies

\[
\boxed{1.5<K_*^{split}<1.7.}
\tag{DT.19}

In particular the lower constraint `K>=K_L` is inactive, `K-delta>0`, and

\[
0<\pi-K-N_*<1.
\]

Thus the smooth formulas above apply at the true minimum.

---

## 8. The feasible `(delta,kappa)` triangle again collapses to one endpoint

Let

\[
G(t,\delta,\kappa)
:=\min_K H(K)-\delta.
\tag{DT.20}

Because the minimizer is unique and interior, the envelope theorem applies.

### `kappa` direction

At fixed `t,delta`,

\[
{\partial N_*\over\partial\kappa}
=-{2\Delta_2\over U^2}<0.
\]

The dominant term has no explicit `kappa` dependence once `K` is held fixed, while
`p=pi-K-N_*` increases with `kappa`.  Therefore

\[
\boxed{
{\partial G\over\partial\kappa}
={2\Delta_2\over U^2}J'(p)>0.
}
\tag{DT.21}

So the weakest point has

\[
\kappa=0.
\]

### `delta` direction

At fixed `t,kappa`,

\[
{\partial N_*\over\partial\delta}
=-2\left(1+{4\Delta_2\over U^2}\right).
\]

Hence at the minimizing split

\[
{\partial G\over\partial\delta}
=-F'(z)
+2\left(1+{4\Delta_2\over U^2}\right)J'(p)-1.
\tag{DT.22}

Since `U>=C0>2sqrt(Delta2)`, the coefficient of `J'` is less than four.  The uniform
split bounds give `p<0.72`, hence

\[
J'(p)<0.058.
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
{\partial G\over\partial\delta}
<4(0.058)-1<0.
}
\tag{DT.23}

The contradiction margin decreases with detour.  The weakest feasible point is
therefore

\[
\boxed{
\kappa=0,
\qquad
\delta={R(t)\over2},
\qquad
U=C_0.
}
\tag{DT.24}

---

## 9. One-variable boundary problem

At (DT.24), define

\[
\delta_0(t)={R(t)\over2},
\tag{DT.25}

\[
N_0(t)
=C_0+{t\over2}-{4\Delta_2\over C_0}.
\tag{DT.26}

Let `K_0(t)` be the unique root in `(1.5,1.7)` of

\[
\boxed{
F'(K_0-\delta_0)
=J'(\pi-K_0-N_0).
}
\tag{DT.27}

The boundary contradiction margin is

\[
\boxed{
G_0(t)
=F(K_0-\delta_0)
+J(\pi-K_0-N_0)
-\delta_0.
}
\tag{DT.28}

It is strictly decreasing.  By the envelope theorem,

\[
G_0'(t)
=-{9\over4}F'(K_0-\delta_0)
-{1\over2}J'(\pi-K_0-N_0)
-{9\over4}<0.
\tag{DT.29}

So one endpoint sign check excludes the entire interval below it.

---

## 10. New floor with `g=0.0050`

At

\[
t=1.83236,
\]

the high-precision certificate gives

\[
\begin{aligned}
\delta_0&=0.0264045395462494157\ldots,\\
N_0&=0.9261669084286363358\ldots,\\
K_0&=1.6442871060685511792\ldots,\\
z_0:=K_0-\delta_0
&=1.6178825665223017635\ldots,\\
p_0:=\pi-K_0-N_0
&=0.5711386390926057234\ldots,\\
F(z_0)&=0.0195347667658293035\ldots,\\
J(p_0)&=0.0068876931759360769\ldots .
\end{aligned}
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
G_0(1.83236)
=0.0000179203955159647\ldots>0.
}
\tag{DT.30}

The scalar root is

\[
\boxed{
1.8323676277438298975236677815\ldots .
}
\tag{DT.31}

Hence:

### Theorem DT.1 — direct dominant/complement energy floor

Every full-core Type-II survivor sequence in the stated proof-only scope satisfies

\[
\boxed{
\liminf_{D\to\infty}{q-k\over D}
\ge1.83236.
}
\tag{DT.32}

This supersedes the standalone `1.82515` joint-orbit floor and the older complement
bracing floor numerically.  Its gain comes from optimizing the **actual allocation of
outer terminal occurrences** between the two disjoint train populations.

---

## 11. Frontier effect

The low-`t` escape is no longer described by a single dominant phase population.  At
the new scalar boundary the minimizing split is explicit:

```text
t                   = 1.83236
outer detour/D       = 0.02640454...
dominant outer ends/D K = 1.64428711...
complement train cap/D  = 0.92616691...
retained complement OO pairs/D = 0.57113864...

dominant energy/D    = 0.01953477...
complement bracing energy/D = 0.00688769...
```

Both terms are macroscopically load-bearing.  A future closure cannot discard the
complement train system as a small error, and it cannot put essentially all outer
terminals into the dominant orbit without making the dominant tangent-mismatch energy
larger.

The next structural target is therefore the **cyclic interaction between these two
large populations**, especially the Woodall diameter-thrackle reuse needed to brace
roughly `0.57D` complement near-antipodal outer trains while the dominant atomic
trains simultaneously consume the rest of the boundary.

C-II remains open.

## 12. Reproducibility

Run

```bash
python3 research/verify_target_c_direct_dominant_complement_energy_tradeoff.py
```

for the high-precision split optimizer, scalar root, and uniform derivative bounds.
